🐳 Day 52 – Docker Images & Containers
🎯 Objective

Learn Docker images and containers, understand the container lifecycle, and practice essential Docker commands for managing images and containers.

📦 Docker Images

A Docker image is a read-only template used to create containers.

An image can contain:

Application code
Dependencies
Libraries
Configuration
Runtime environment

Example:

Nginx Image
    │
    ├── Container 1
    ├── Container 2
    └── Container 3

One image can be used to create multiple containers.
🐳 Docker Containers

A container is a running or stopped instance created from a Docker image.

Docker Image
     ↓
Docker Container

Containers provide:

Process isolation
Portability
Lightweight execution
Fast startup
Consistent environments
🔄 Container Lifecycle

A simplified lifecycle:

Created
   ↓
Running
   ↓
Stopped
   ↓
Removed

Common commands:

docker create
docker run
docker stop
docker start
docker restart
docker rm
📥 Docker Image Commands
Pull an Image
docker pull nginx

Downloads the Nginx image from a configured registry such as Docker Hub.

List Images
docker images

or:

docker image ls
Remove an Image
docker rmi nginx

If the image is still referenced by existing containers, Docker may prevent removal until those references are handled.

Tag an Image
docker tag nginx mynginx:v1

Tags provide human-readable names and versions for images.
